const q3 = quad(1, 2, 5), rt = q3.roots[0];
must(q3.D.n === -16n && ceq(rt, C(-1, 2)) && ceq(cadd(cadd(cmul(rt, rt), cmul(C(2), rt)), C(5)), C(0)), 'WE3 root check');
const we3Table = table(['Step', 'Working'], [['Discriminant', r`$D = 2^2 - 4(1)(5) = -16$`], ['Roots', r`$x = \dfrac{-2 \pm \sqrt{-16}}{2} = \dfrac{-2 \pm 4i}{2} = -1 \pm 2i$`], ['Check $x = -1 + 2i$', `$(-1 + 2i)^2 + 2(-1 + 2i) + 5 = (-3 - 4i) + (-2 + 4i) + 5 = 0$`]], 'Table solving x squared plus 2 x plus 5 equal 0: the discriminant is minus 16, the roots are minus 1 plus or minus 2 i, and substituting minus 1 plus 2 i gives zero.', r`$x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0$.`);
must(ceq(cmul(rt, rt), C(-3, -4)), '(-1 + 2i)^2 = -3 - 4i');
const summary2 = flow('LR', [['a', r`Argand plane: $a + bi$ is $(a, b)$`], ['m', r`Modulus $|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$; conjugate mirrors`], ['p', 'Polar form: $r(\\cos\\theta + i\\sin\\theta)$ (formative)'], ['q', 'Negative discriminant: conjugate roots (formative)']], [['a', 'm'], ['m', 'p'], ['p', 'q']], 'Summary flow: complex numbers are points of the Argand plane; the modulus is the distance from the origin and the conjugate is the mirror image; the polar form uses modulus and argument; a quadratic with negative discriminant has a conjugate pair of roots.', 'Argand plane, modulus, polar form and quadratics.');

// ================================================================ deck 1
const d1 = deck({
  part: 1,
  title: 'Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations — Part 1: Complex numbers and their algebra',
  goals: ['Explain why real numbers are not enough and define the imaginary unit', 'Write a complex number as $a + bi$ and read its real and imaginary parts', 'Use the cycle of powers of $i$', 'Add, subtract, multiply and divide complex numbers', 'Use the conjugate and the equality of complex numbers'],
  slides: [
    sl('title', 'Complex Numbers, Part 1: Algebra of complex numbers', { b: 'Some equations have no real solution. What new numbers make them solvable?', v: numberSystems, n: 'Welcome. Every time mathematicians met an equation with no answer, they enlarged the numbers. Whole numbers became integers, then fractions, then all real numbers. One more step remains: an equation like x squared plus one equals zero has no real root. In this part you meet complex numbers and learn to add, multiply and divide them.' }),
    sl('concept', 'What you will be able to do', { ul: ['Define the **imaginary unit** $i$ with $i^2 = -1$', 'Write numbers as **$a + bi$** and name the parts', 'Use the **cycle of powers** of $i$', 'Add, subtract, multiply and **divide** complex numbers', 'Use the **conjugate** and equal complex numbers'], n: 'Here are your goals. You will define the imaginary unit, write numbers in the form a plus b i, and use the repeating cycle of its powers. You will add, subtract, multiply and divide complex numbers, using the conjugate for division. Finally you will solve for unknown parts when two complex numbers are equal. These appear as objective and short answer questions.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Why we need new numbers', { b: r`The graph of $y = x^2 + 1$ stays above the $x$-axis, so $x^2 + 1 = 0$ has **no real root**. We invent a number $i$ with $i^2 = -1$ so that it has roots $\pm i$.`, v: pNoRoot, n: 'Look at the parabola y equals x squared plus one. It never reaches the x axis, because x squared is never negative. So the equation x squared plus one equals zero has no real solution. We fix this by inventing a new number, called i, whose square is minus one. Then the equation has two roots, i and minus i.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The imaginary unit and the form a + bi', { b: r`$i^2 = -1$. A **complex number** is $z = a + bi$ with $a, b$ real: $a = \mathrm{Re}(z)$ is the real part and $b = \mathrm{Im}(z)$ the imaginary part.`, v: defTable, m: [r`z = a + bi,\quad i^2 = -1`], n: 'A complex number is written a plus b i, where a and b are real numbers. The number a is the real part and b is the imaginary part. Note that the imaginary part is just b, without the letter i. A number with imaginary part zero is real, and one with real part zero is purely imaginary. Real numbers are special complex numbers.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Powers of i', { b: r`The powers of $i$ repeat every four steps: $i, -1, -i, 1$. Divide the exponent by $4$ and keep the remainder.`, v: powTable, n: 'Start with i. Then i squared is minus one, i cubed is minus i, and i to the fourth is one. After that the pattern repeats: i to the fifth is i again. So to find a large power, divide the exponent by four and look at the remainder. A remainder of zero gives one, one gives i, two gives minus one, and three gives minus i.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Reading the remainder', { b: r`$i^{n} = i^{r}$ where $r$ is the remainder of $n$ divided by $4$. Negative powers work too: $i^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{i} = -i$.`, v: remTable, m: [r`i^{4k + r} = i^{r}`], n: 'Here is the rule as a table. For example, i to the power thirty-five: thirty-five leaves remainder three on division by four, so the value is i cubed, which is minus i. Negative exponents follow the same cycle. One over i is minus i, because multiplying top and bottom by i gives i over minus one.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a power of i', { b: 'What is the value of i to the power thirty-five?', chk: 'fb1', n: 'Divide thirty-five by four and use the remainder.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Adding and subtracting', { b: r`Add or subtract the real parts and the imaginary parts separately: $(a + bi) \pm (c + di) = (a \pm c) + (b \pm d)i$.`, v: addTable, m: [r`(a + bi) \pm (c + di) = (a \pm c) + (b \pm d)i`], n: 'Adding complex numbers is like collecting like terms. Add the real parts together and the imaginary parts together. With five plus two i and three minus six i, the sum is eight minus four i. For subtraction, subtract each part. Be careful with signs: subtracting minus six i adds six i, giving two plus eight i.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Multiplying', { b: r`Multiply like brackets and replace $i^2$ by $-1$: $(a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i$.`, v: mulTable, m: [r`(a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i`], n: 'To multiply, expand the brackets as usual. You get four terms. The term with i squared becomes minus one times its coefficient. In the table, four plus three i times two minus five i expands to eight, minus twenty i, plus six i, minus fifteen i squared. The last term becomes plus fifteen, and the answer is twenty-three minus fourteen i.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 1: a product and a sum', { b: r`Simplify $(4 - i)(2 + 5i) + (3 + 2i)$ and write the answer as $a + bi$.`, ul: [r`**Product —** $8 + 20i - 2i - 5i^2 = 13 + 18i$`, r`**Add —** $(13 + 18i) + (3 + 2i) = 16 + 20i$`, r`**Answer —** $16 + 20i$`], v: we1Table, n: 'First expand the product. Four times two is eight, four times five i is twenty i, minus i times two is minus two i, and minus i times five i is minus five i squared, which is plus five. So the product is thirteen plus eighteen i. Now add three plus two i. The real parts give sixteen and the imaginary parts give twenty. The answer is sixteen plus twenty i.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a product', { b: 'Multiply five minus two i by three plus four i.', chk: 'm05', n: 'Expand, then replace i squared by minus one. Do not drop that last term.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The conjugate', { b: r`The **conjugate** of $z = a + bi$ is $\bar z = a - bi$. Then $z + \bar z = 2a$ and $z\bar z = a^2 + b^2$ are both real.`, v: conjTable, m: [r`\bar z = a - bi,\quad z\bar z = a^2 + b^2`], n: 'The conjugate of a complex number keeps the real part and flips the sign of the imaginary part. The table shows four examples. In every row the sum of a number and its conjugate is real, and so is their product. The product equals the sum of the squares of the two parts, which is never negative. This product is the key to division.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Dividing: use the conjugate', { b: r`To divide, multiply top and bottom by the **conjugate of the divisor**. The denominator becomes the real number $c^2 + d^2$.`, v: divRule, m: [r`\dfrac{a + bi}{c + di} = \dfrac{(a + bi)(c - di)}{c^2 + d^2}`], n: 'To divide by a complex number, multiply top and bottom by its conjugate. The new denominator is real, because a number times its conjugate is the sum of two squares. Here we divide seven plus four i by three minus two i. The numerator becomes thirteen plus twenty-six i, the denominator is thirteen, and the quotient is one plus two i. Check by multiplying back.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 2: a quotient', { b: r`Express $\dfrac{7 + 4i}{3 - 2i}$ in the form $a + bi$.`, ul: [r`**Conjugate of the divisor —** $3 + 2i$`, r`**Numerator —** $(7 + 4i)(3 + 2i) = 13 + 26i$`, r`**Denominator —** $9 + 4 = 13$`, r`**Answer —** $1 + 2i$`], v: divTable, n: 'We multiply top and bottom by three plus two i. The numerator expands to twenty-one plus fourteen i plus twelve i plus eight i squared, which is thirteen plus twenty-six i. The denominator is nine plus four, which is thirteen. Dividing each part by thirteen gives one plus two i. To be safe, multiply one plus two i by three minus two i and confirm you get seven plus four i.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a high power', { b: 'Evaluate one minus i to the power eight.', chk: 'n06', n: 'Square it first and see what you get.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Equal complex numbers', { b: r`Two complex numbers are equal when their **real parts are equal** and their **imaginary parts are equal**.`, v: eqRule, n: 'One complex equation gives two real equations. If a plus i b equals c plus i d, then a equals c and b equals d. So an equation with two unknown real numbers can be solved by comparing the real parts and the imaginary parts separately. This is the standard way to find unknown letters in complex number problems.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 3: find x and y', { b: r`Find real $x$ and $y$ if $(2x - y) + i(x + 3y) = 3 + 5i$.`, ul: [r`**Real parts —** $2x - y = 3$`, r`**Imaginary parts —** $x + 3y = 5$`, r`**Solve —** $y = 2x - 3$, so $7x - 9 = 5$, $x = 2$, $y = 1$`], v: eqTable, n: 'Compare real parts: two x minus y equals three. Compare imaginary parts: x plus three y equals five. From the first, y equals two x minus three. Substitute into the second to get seven x minus nine equals five, so x is two and y is one. Check by substituting back: both sides become three plus five i.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a true or false', { b: 'True or false: the product of two non-real complex numbers can be real.', chk: 'tf1', n: 'Try a number and its conjugate.' }),
    sl('summary', 'Summary', { ul: [r`$i^2 = -1$; powers of $i$ repeat in a cycle of four`, r`$a + bi$: real part $a$, imaginary part $b$`, r`Add and subtract part by part; multiply like brackets`, r`$\bar z = a - bi$; $z\bar z = a^2 + b^2$`, 'Divide using the conjugate of the divisor; equal numbers have equal parts'], v: summary1, n: 'To sum up. The imaginary unit squares to minus one and its powers cycle with period four. Complex numbers add and subtract part by part and multiply like brackets. The conjugate flips the sign of the imaginary part, and a number times its conjugate is real, which is how we divide. Two complex numbers are equal when both their parts match.' }),
    sl('exam-tips', 'Exam tips: how marks are awarded', { ul: ['**Equal numbers (1–2 marks):** two equations, two values', '**Product or quotient (2–3 marks):** expansion, conjugate step, final $a + bi$', '**Powers of $i$ (1–2 marks):** remainder on division by $4$', '**Mistake:** forgetting that $i^2 = -1$ in a product', '**Mistake:** writing the imaginary part as $bi$ instead of $b$', '**Mistake:** dividing without the conjugate'], n: 'In product and quotient questions, marks go for the expansion, for using the conjugate of the divisor, and for the final answer in the form a plus b i. In equality questions you earn a mark for each equation. Watch for three slips: forgetting that i squared is minus one, calling the imaginary part b i instead of b, and dividing without multiplying by the conjugate.' }),
  ],
});

// ================================================================ deck 2
const d2 = deck({
  part: 2,
  title: 'Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations — Part 2: Argand plane, modulus, polar form and quadratic equations',
  goals: ['Represent complex numbers on the Argand plane', 'Find the modulus and the conjugate and use their properties', 'Interpret the conjugate and the sum geometrically', 'Write a complex number in polar form (formative)', 'Solve quadratics with real coefficients and complex roots (formative)'],
  slides: [
    sl('title', 'Complex Numbers, Part 2: The Argand plane', { b: 'Every complex number is a point of a plane. What does its size and direction tell us?', v: pPlane, n: 'Welcome to part two. A complex number a plus b i can be drawn as the point a comma b, with the real part across and the imaginary part up. This is the Argand plane. In this part you will read distance and direction from the picture, meet the modulus and conjugate as geometric ideas, and see how complex numbers connect to quadratic equations.' }),
    sl('concept', 'What you will be able to do', { ul: ['Plot complex numbers on the **Argand plane**', 'Find the **modulus** and **conjugate** and use their rules', 'See the **conjugate** as a mirror image and the sum as a parallelogram', 'Write the **polar form** (formative only)', 'Solve **quadratics** with complex roots (formative only)'], n: 'Here are your goals. You will plot complex numbers and find their modulus and conjugate. You will use the rules for the modulus of products and quotients, and see the conjugate and the sum as pictures. The polar form and quadratic equations with complex roots are formative topics: they are taught for understanding and are not assessed in the annual board paper.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The Argand plane', { b: r`$a + bi$ is the point $(a, b)$: **real axis** across, **imaginary axis** up. The sign pair tells the quadrant.`, v: pPlane, n: 'In the Argand plane the horizontal axis holds the real part and the vertical axis holds the imaginary part. The number three plus two i is three across and two up, in the first quadrant. Minus two plus three i is in the second quadrant, minus three minus i is in the third, and two minus three i is in the fourth. Real numbers lie on the horizontal axis.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: which quadrant?', { b: 'In which quadrant does the reciprocal of minus two plus i lie?', chk: 'm13', n: 'Rationalise first, then read the signs of the two parts.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The conjugate as a mirror image', { b: r`$\bar z$ is the reflection of $z$ in the **real axis**: same real part, opposite imaginary part. $z$ and $\bar z$ are equally far from the origin.`, v: pConj, n: 'On the Argand plane the conjugate of a number is its mirror image in the real axis. Four plus three i sits three units above the axis, and its conjugate four minus three i sits three units below it. The two points are the same distance from the origin. This picture explains why a number and its conjugate have the same modulus.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The modulus', { b: r`$|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$ is the distance of $z = a + bi$ from the origin. $|z| = 5$ describes a **circle** about the origin.`, v: pRing, m: [r`|a + bi| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}`], n: 'The modulus of a complex number is its distance from the origin, found with the Pythagorean theorem. The number three plus four i has modulus the square root of nine plus sixteen, which is five. Every point at distance five from the origin lies on one circle. So the equation modulus of z equals five describes a circle with centre at the origin and radius five.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Rules for the modulus', { b: r`$|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$, $\left|\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}\right| = \dfrac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$, $|\bar z| = |z|$ and $z\bar z = |z|^2$.`, v: propsTable, n: 'The modulus behaves well with products and quotients: the modulus of a product is the product of the moduli, and the modulus of a quotient is the quotient of the moduli. A number and its conjugate have the same modulus, and a number times its conjugate equals the modulus squared. The table checks each rule for two and three specific numbers.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 1: modulus of a product', { b: r`Find $|(2 + i)(3 - 2i)|$ in two ways.`, ul: [r`**Expand —** $(2 + i)(3 - 2i) = 8 - i$`, r`**Directly —** $\sqrt{64 + 1} = \sqrt{65}$`, r`**By the rule —** $\sqrt{5}\times\sqrt{13} = \sqrt{65}$`], v: we1Mod, n: 'Method one: multiply out. Two plus i times three minus two i gives eight minus i. Its modulus is the square root of sixty-four plus one, which is the square root of sixty-five. Method two: use the rule. The modulus of two plus i is root five, and the modulus of three minus two i is root thirteen. Their product is root sixty-five. The two methods agree, and the rule is much quicker for long products.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a distance', { b: 'How far from the origin is the point nine minus twelve i?', chk: 'fb3', n: 'Use the square root of the sum of squares.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Addition as a parallelogram', { b: r`$z_1 + z_2$ is the fourth corner of the parallelogram on $0$, $z_1$ and $z_2$. Hence $|z_1 + z_2| \le |z_1| + |z_2|$ (the **triangle inequality**).`, v: pPara, n: 'To add complex numbers on the plane, complete the parallelogram. Two plus three i and three minus i have the sum five plus two i, the fourth corner. The diagonal from the origin is shorter than the two sides together, which is the triangle inequality: the modulus of a sum is at most the sum of the moduli. Equality holds only when the two numbers point in the same direction.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Polar form (formative only)', { b: r`$z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$ with $r = |z|$ and $\theta$ the **argument**, the angle from the positive real axis. *Taught for understanding; not assessed in the annual paper.*`, v: polFig, m: [r`z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)`], n: 'A point can be described by its distance from the origin and its direction. The distance is the modulus r. The direction is the argument, the angle theta that the line to the point makes with the positive real axis. For one plus root three i, the modulus is two and the angle is sixty degrees. The polar form is r times cosine theta plus i sine theta. This topic is for understanding and is not assessed in the annual paper.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The argument by position', { b: r`The **principal argument** lies in $(-180^\circ, 180^\circ]$. Use the quadrant: find the related angle $\alpha$ with $\tan\alpha = \dfrac{|b|}{|a|}$, then place it.`, v: argTable, n: 'The principal argument is measured anticlockwise from the positive real axis and lies between minus one hundred eighty and one hundred eighty degrees. First find the related acute angle using the tangent of the absolute values. Then adjust for the quadrant. Points on the axes have the arguments zero, ninety, one hundred eighty and minus ninety degrees.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 2: a polar form', { b: r`Write $-1 - \sqrt{3}\,i$ in polar form.`, ul: [r`**Modulus —** $r = \sqrt{1 + 3} = 2$`, r`**Quadrant —** third; related angle $60^\circ$`, r`**Argument —** $-180^\circ + 60^\circ = -120^\circ$`, r`**Polar form —** $2\left(\cos(-120^\circ) + i\sin(-120^\circ)\right)$`], v: we2Table, n: 'First the modulus: the square root of one plus three is two. Both parts are negative, so the point is in the third quadrant. The tangent of the related angle is root three, so the related angle is sixty degrees. In the third quadrant the principal argument is minus one hundred eighty plus sixty, which is minus one hundred twenty degrees. The polar form follows.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: an argument', { b: 'True or false: the principal argument of minus four i is minus ninety degrees.', chk: 'tf6', n: 'Which axis is minus four i on?' }),
    sl('concept', 'Quadratic equations with complex roots (formative only)', { b: r`If $b^2 - 4ac < 0$ (real $a, b, c$), then $x = \dfrac{-b \pm i\sqrt{4ac - b^2}}{2a}$: two **non-real roots, conjugate** to each other.`, v: quadTable, m: [r`x = \dfrac{-b \pm i\sqrt{4ac - b^2}}{2a}`], n: 'The quadratic formula now works for every real quadratic. When the discriminant is negative, its square root is i times a real number, so the two roots are non-real. They are conjugates of each other. The table shows three equations with negative discriminants. Solving quadratics over complex numbers is a formative topic and is not assessed in the annual paper.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 3: solving a quadratic', { b: r`Solve $x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0$ and check one root.`, ul: [r`**Discriminant —** $4 - 20 = -16$`, r`**Roots —** $\dfrac{-2 \pm 4i}{2} = -1 \pm 2i$`, r`**Check —** $(-1 + 2i)^2 + 2(-1 + 2i) + 5 = 0$`], v: we3Table, n: 'The discriminant is four minus twenty, which is minus sixteen, so the roots are not real. The square root of minus sixteen is four i. The roots are minus two plus or minus four i, all over two, giving minus one plus or minus two i. To check, square minus one plus two i to get minus three minus four i, add minus two plus four i, and add five. The total is zero.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: modulus of a root', { b: 'Find the common modulus of the two roots of x squared minus ten x plus one hundred sixty-nine.', chk: 'n05', n: 'Solve the quadratic first, then take the modulus.' }),
    sl('summary', 'Summary', { ul: [r`$a + bi$ is the point $(a, b)$ of the Argand plane`, r`$|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$; $\bar z$ is the mirror image in the real axis`, r`$|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$; $z\bar z = |z|^2$; $|z_1 + z_2| \le |z_1| + |z_2|$`, r`Polar form $r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$ (formative)`, 'Negative discriminant: a conjugate pair of non-real roots (formative)'], v: summary2, n: 'To sum up. Complex numbers are points of the Argand plane. The modulus is the distance from the origin and the conjugate is the mirror image in the real axis. The modulus of a product is the product of the moduli. The polar form uses the modulus and the argument. And a real quadratic with a negative discriminant has two conjugate roots that are not real.' }),
    sl('exam-tips', 'Exam tips: how marks are awarded', { ul: ['**Modulus and conjugate (1–2 marks):** formula, exact surd, correct sign', '**Argand plane (1–2 marks):** correct quadrant from the signs', '**Property verification (3 marks):** both sides computed separately', '**Mistake:** $|a + bi| = a + b$ instead of $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$', '**Mistake:** giving the related angle instead of the argument', '**Light topics:** polar form and complex quadratics are formative only'], n: 'For modulus questions, write the formula, keep the answer as an exact square root, and mind the signs. For the Argand plane, read the quadrant from the signs of the two parts. In verification questions compute both sides separately. Avoid three slips: adding a and b instead of using squares, giving the related angle instead of the argument, and spending long on polar form and complex quadratics, which are formative only.' }),
  ],
});

// ================================================================ notes
const notes = {
  unit: 'cbse-11-mathematics-ch04', version: 1, title: 'Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations — chapter notes', language: 'en',
  sections: [
    { heading: '1. Need for complex numbers; the imaginary unit', body_md: r`The equation $x^2 + 1 = 0$ has no real root because $x^2 \ge 0$. We introduce the **imaginary unit** $i$ with $i^2 = -1$.\n\nA **complex number** is $z = a + bi$ with $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$: $a = \mathrm{Re}(z)$, $b = \mathrm{Im}(z)$. If $b = 0$ it is real; if $a = 0$ and $b \ne 0$ it is purely imaginary. Every real number is complex.\n\n**Powers of $i$:** $i, -1, -i, 1$ repeat in a cycle of four, so $i^{n} = i^{r}$ where $r$ is the remainder of $n$ on division by $4$ (also for negative $n$: $i^{-1} = -i$). Any four consecutive powers add to $0$.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Imaginary unit', definition_md: r`The number $i$ with $i^2 = -1$.` }, { term: 'Complex number', definition_md: r`A number $a + bi$ with $a, b$ real.` }, { term: 'Real and imaginary part', definition_md: r`$a$ and $b$ (the imaginary part is the real number $b$, not $bi$).` }], formulas: [{ latex: r`i^2 = -1,\quad i^{4k + r} = i^{r}`, label: 'Powers of i' }], visual: pNoRoot, visuals: [defTable, powTable, remTable], topic_ids: [topic(1)] },
    { heading: '2. Equality, addition, subtraction and multiplication', body_md: r`**Equality:** $a + bi = c + di \iff a = c$ and $b = d$. One complex equation gives two real equations.\n\n**Sum and difference:** $(a + bi) \pm (c + di) = (a \pm c) + (b \pm d)i$.\n\n**Product:** expand and use $i^2 = -1$: $(a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i$.\n\nAddition and multiplication are commutative and associative, and multiplication distributes over addition, exactly as for real numbers. There is **no ordering** of complex numbers: $2 + 3i > 1 + 2i$ has no meaning.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Equal complex numbers', definition_md: 'Same real part and same imaginary part.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`(a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i`, label: 'Product' }, { latex: r`a + ib = c + id \iff a = c \text{ and } b = d`, label: 'Equality' }], visual: addTable, visuals: [mulTable, we1Table, eqRule, eqTable], topic_ids: [topic(1)] },
    { heading: '3. Conjugate, reciprocal and division', body_md: r`The **conjugate** of $z = a + bi$ is $\bar z = a - bi$.\n\n- $z + \bar z = 2a$ (real) and $z - \bar z = 2bi$ (purely imaginary).\n- $z\bar z = a^2 + b^2$ (real, non-negative).\n- $\bar{\bar z} = z$; $z$ is real iff $\bar z = z$.\n\n**Reciprocal:** $\dfrac{1}{z} = \dfrac{\bar z}{a^2 + b^2}$ for $z \ne 0$.\n\n**Division:** multiply top and bottom by the conjugate of the divisor: $\dfrac{a + bi}{c + di} = \dfrac{(a + bi)(c - di)}{c^2 + d^2}$. Check by multiplying the quotient by the divisor.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Conjugate', definition_md: r`$\bar z = a - bi$.` }, { term: 'Multiplicative inverse', definition_md: r`$\dfrac{1}{z} = \dfrac{\bar z}{|z|^2}$.` }], formulas: [{ latex: r`z\bar z = a^2 + b^2`, label: 'Conjugate product' }, { latex: r`\frac{1}{a + bi} = \frac{a - bi}{a^2 + b^2}`, label: 'Reciprocal' }], visual: conjTable, visuals: [divTable], topic_ids: [topic(1), topic(2)] },
    { heading: '4. The Argand plane', body_md: r`The complex number $a + bi$ is the point $(a, b)$: the **real axis** is horizontal and the **imaginary axis** vertical. Real numbers lie on the real axis and purely imaginary numbers on the imaginary axis.\n\nThe sign pair $(\mathrm{Re}, \mathrm{Im})$ gives the quadrant: $(+, +)$ first, $(-, +)$ second, $(-, -)$ third, $(+, -)$ fourth.\n\nThe conjugate $\bar z$ is the mirror image of $z$ in the real axis; $-z$ is the image in the origin; the sum $z_1 + z_2$ is the fourth corner of the parallelogram on $0, z_1, z_2$.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Argand plane', definition_md: 'The plane whose points represent complex numbers.' }, { term: 'Real axis, imaginary axis', definition_md: 'The horizontal and vertical axes.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`a + bi \leftrightarrow (a, b)`, label: 'Representation' }], visual: pPlane, visuals: [pConj, pPara], topic_ids: [topic(2)] },
    { heading: '5. The modulus and its properties', body_md: r`The **modulus** of $z = a + bi$ is $|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$, the distance from the origin; keep exact surds.\n\n- $|z| \ge 0$, and $|z| = 0$ only for $z = 0$.\n- $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$ and $\left|\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}\right| = \dfrac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$.\n- $|\bar z| = |z|$ and $z\bar z = |z|^2$.\n- $|z_1 + z_2| \le |z_1| + |z_2|$ (triangle inequality).\n\n$|z| = k$ is the circle with centre at the origin and radius $k$. The moduli of complex numbers can be compared; the numbers themselves cannot.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Modulus', definition_md: r`$|z| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$, the distance from the origin.` }, { term: 'Triangle inequality', definition_md: r`$|z_1 + z_2| \le |z_1| + |z_2|$.` }], formulas: [{ latex: r`|a + bi| = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}`, label: 'Modulus' }, { latex: r`|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|`, label: 'Product rule' }], visual: pRing, visuals: [propsTable, we1Mod], topic_ids: [topic(2)] },
    { heading: '6. Polar form and argument (formative)', body_md: r`*Formative only: not assessed in the annual paper.*\n\nWrite $z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$ with $r = |z|$ and $\theta$ the **argument**, the angle from the positive real axis to the line from the origin to $z$ (anticlockwise positive). The **principal argument** lies in $(-180^\circ, 180^\circ]$.\n\nTo find $\theta$: take the related angle $\alpha$ with $\tan\alpha = \dfrac{|b|}{|a|}$ and place it using the quadrant. Examples: $1 + i$ has $\theta = 45^\circ$; $-1 - i$ has $-135^\circ$; $-4$ has $180^\circ$; $-4i$ has $-90^\circ$.\n\nCheck a polar form by expanding: $r\cos\theta = a$ and $r\sin\theta = b$.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Argument', definition_md: 'The angle of the point from the positive real axis.' }, { term: 'Polar form', definition_md: r`$r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$.` }], formulas: [{ latex: r`z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)`, label: 'Polar form' }, { latex: r`r\cos\theta = a,\quad r\sin\theta = b`, label: 'Back to a + bi' }], visual: polFig, visuals: [argTable, we2Table], topic_ids: [topic(3)] },
    { heading: '7. Quadratic equations with complex roots (formative)', body_md: r`*Formative only: not assessed in the annual paper.*\n\nFor $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ with real coefficients and discriminant $D = b^2 - 4ac$:\n\n- $D > 0$: two distinct real roots; $D = 0$: equal real roots;\n- $D < 0$: two **non-real roots** $\dfrac{-b \pm i\sqrt{-D}}{2a}$, which are **conjugates** of each other.\n\nThe sum of the roots is $-\dfrac{b}{a}$ and the product is $\dfrac{c}{a}$. For a conjugate pair the product equals the square of the common modulus. A real quadratic never has exactly one non-real root.\n\nCheck a root by substitution.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Discriminant', definition_md: r`$D = b^2 - 4ac$.` }, { term: 'Conjugate roots', definition_md: r`The two non-real roots of a real quadratic: $p \pm iq$.` }], formulas: [{ latex: r`x = \frac{-b \pm i\sqrt{4ac - b^2}}{2a}\quad (D < 0)`, label: 'Complex roots' }], visual: quadTable, visuals: [we3Table], topic_ids: [topic(3)] },
  ],
  exam_tips_md: r`**How marks are awarded**\n- *Equal complex numbers (1–2 marks):* two real equations, then $x$ and $y$.\n- *Product or quotient (2–3 marks):* expansion, conjugate of the divisor, final $a + bi$.\n- *Powers of $i$ (1–2 marks):* reduce the exponent modulo $4$.\n- *Modulus and conjugate (1–2 marks):* formula, exact surd, correct signs.\n- *Verification of a property (3 marks):* each side computed separately.\n- *Argand plane (1–2 marks):* point, quadrant or mirror image.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n1. Forgetting $i^2 = -1$ when multiplying.\n2. Writing the imaginary part as $bi$ instead of $b$.\n3. Dividing without multiplying by the conjugate of the divisor.\n4. $|a + bi| = a + b$ instead of $\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$.\n5. $\sqrt{-a}\sqrt{-b} = \sqrt{ab}$: the rule needs non-negative $a$ and $b$.\n6. Treating $2 + 3i > 1 + 2i$ as meaningful: complex numbers are not ordered.\n7. Giving the related angle as the argument.\n8. Polar form and complex quadratics are formative only: do not over-invest.`,
  status: 'draft', provenance: d1.provenance,
};

